1
00:00:07,079 --> 00:00:08,999
Welcome to Web sixty four.

2
00:00:10,079 --> 00:00:15,783
In this tutorial, we will make Aurora: a colourful character plasma with a custom

3
00:00:15,783 --> 00:00:18,228
character set and a scrolling message.

4
00:00:19,307 --> 00:00:25,314
Everything belongs to one native Web sixty four project, so the source, graphics, and

5
00:00:25,314 --> 00:00:27,030
build settings travel together.

6
00:00:28,109 --> 00:00:33,203
We will use C for setup and text, and a small sixty-five-oh-two assembly loop

7
00:00:33,203 --> 00:00:34,659
for the animated field.

8
00:00:35,738 --> 00:00:38,810
You do not need to type hundreds of lines while watching.

9
00:00:39,889 --> 00:00:44,305
The accompanying source and character set are supplied teaching files.

10
00:00:45,385 --> 00:00:51,189
We will import them, examine the important parts, make a small edit, and run

11
00:00:51,189 --> 00:00:54,921
the result in the integrated Commodore sixty four emulator.

12
00:00:57,204 --> 00:00:58,719
Begin in the Code tab.

13
00:00:59,923 --> 00:01:02,526
The file tree is our view of the project.

14
00:01:03,730 --> 00:01:06,076
Open New and choose C project.

15
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:11,928
Web sixty four creates a C starter with a main function and its own

16
00:01:11,928 --> 00:01:12,592
build target.

17
00:01:13,796 --> 00:01:19,511
The template note explains the starting structure; main dot C is the program entry

18
00:01:19,511 --> 00:01:19,919
source.

19
00:01:21,123 --> 00:01:25,454
Save the project early, using the project save control at the top.

20
00:01:26,658 --> 00:01:28,855
I am calling this Plasma Tutorial.

21
00:01:30,059 --> 00:01:34,283
The web sixty four proj file is the complete working project.

22
00:01:35,487 --> 00:01:40,124
Saving only a C source file would not preserve the character set and target

23
00:01:40,124 --> 00:01:42,442
settings that we are about to add.

24
00:01:43,646 --> 00:01:45,780
There are two useful distinctions here.

25
00:01:46,984 --> 00:01:49,160
A source file is something we edit.

26
00:01:50,364 --> 00:01:54,801
A build target describes what the IDE produces from those files.

27
00:01:56,005 --> 00:01:59,504
The final P R G is the executable result.

28
00:02:00,708 --> 00:02:05,797
Keep the native project as your editable master, and export the P R G

29
00:02:05,797 --> 00:02:09,796
when you want to distribute or run the built program elsewhere.

30
00:02:12,129 --> 00:02:17,782
Now import the supplied Aurora dot C H R file using Import, Add files.

31
00:02:18,911 --> 00:02:24,436
This is a raw character set: two thousand and forty-eight bytes, or two hundred

32
00:02:24,436 --> 00:02:27,594
and fifty-six characters with eight bytes per character.

33
00:02:28,723 --> 00:02:33,779
Once imported, it becomes a native editable graphics asset in the project.

34
00:02:34,908 --> 00:02:36,572
Open the Char Editor.

35
00:02:37,700 --> 00:02:41,412
Each thumbnail represents an eight by eight pixel character.

36
00:02:42,541 --> 00:02:47,858
The large grid edits one character, while the properties describe its index and video

37
00:02:47,858 --> 00:02:48,237
mode.

38
00:02:49,366 --> 00:02:54,337
Our set contains a small uppercase font and sixteen ordered-dither patterns.

39
00:02:55,466 --> 00:02:58,751
The font gives the demonstration its own lettering.

40
00:02:59,880 --> 00:03:04,168
The patterns turn a simple character grid into a textured display.

41
00:03:05,297 --> 00:03:07,644
This project uses text hires mode.

42
00:03:08,772 --> 00:03:14,447
Each character pixel is either background or foreground; the foreground colour can still vary

43
00:03:14,447 --> 00:03:16,879
from screen cell to screen cell.

44
00:03:18,008 --> 00:03:20,611
We do not need bitmap mode for this effect.

45
00:03:21,740 --> 00:03:26,916
Select a letter, then a dither pattern, and compare the large pixel grid with

46
00:03:26,916 --> 00:03:28,396
the repeated tile preview.

47
00:03:29,524 --> 00:03:32,170
For your own version, edit the graphic here.

48
00:03:33,299 --> 00:03:37,117
Web sixty four updates the generated bindings for you.

49
00:03:38,246 --> 00:03:42,961
Do not maintain a second handwritten copy of the generated include.

50
00:03:44,090 --> 00:03:49,486
Save the project again so that the editable asset and its source references stay

51
00:03:49,486 --> 00:03:49,871
together.

52
00:03:51,442 --> 00:03:54,813
Before adding the full program, set up the build target.

53
00:03:55,255 --> 00:03:59,394
Name the target Aurora and the output Aurora dot P R G.

54
00:03:59,836 --> 00:04:02,886
Set Origin to hexadecimal four thousand.

55
00:04:03,329 --> 00:04:08,001
The dollar sign is the assembler notation for a hexadecimal address.

56
00:04:08,443 --> 00:04:11,238
Our memory layout is deliberately simple.

57
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:15,605
Screen memory starts at hexadecimal zero four hundred.

58
00:04:16,047 --> 00:04:18,671
Colour R A M is at D eight hundred.

59
00:04:19,113 --> 00:04:22,740
The custom character set will be copied to three eight hundred.

60
00:04:23,182 --> 00:04:28,793
The program begins at four thousand, immediately after that two-kilobyte character area.

61
00:04:29,235 --> 00:04:31,454
These addresses have different jobs.

62
00:04:31,896 --> 00:04:36,930
The program's load address does not choose where the video chip finds its characters.

63
00:04:37,373 --> 00:04:42,087
We will configure the VIC-two memory register separately in the source.

64
00:04:42,530 --> 00:04:48,639
Keeping those two decisions clear prevents a common error: loading graphics successfully but telling

65
00:04:48,639 --> 00:04:52,130
the video chip to display some other memory.

66
00:04:52,572 --> 00:04:57,164
The root source stays main dot C, and the target remains a P R

67
00:04:57,164 --> 00:04:57,820
G program.

68
00:04:58,262 --> 00:05:04,270
This example needs no disk mastering step: its code and character data fit in

69
00:05:04,270 --> 00:05:05,558
a single executable.

70
00:05:06,351 --> 00:05:09,509
Import plasma dot I N C using Add files.

71
00:05:09,860 --> 00:05:13,700
Then open main dot C and paste the supplied C source.

72
00:05:14,051 --> 00:05:17,273
These teaching files are included with the tutorial.

73
00:05:17,624 --> 00:05:20,099
The C program handles setup and text.

74
00:05:20,450 --> 00:05:25,975
Its plasma frame function includes the assembly kernel, which we can inspect as a

75
00:05:25,975 --> 00:05:26,765
separate file.

76
00:05:27,116 --> 00:05:31,660
The effect begins with a table of two hundred and fifty-six sine samples.

77
00:05:32,011 --> 00:05:34,571
The values range from zero to thirty-one.

78
00:05:34,923 --> 00:05:40,514
We are using a lookup table instead of calculating trigonometry on the Commodore sixty

79
00:05:40,514 --> 00:05:42,112
four for every cell.

80
00:05:42,463 --> 00:05:46,623
Advancing a byte-sized phase naturally wraps around the table.

81
00:05:46,975 --> 00:05:51,817
First, the code calculates forty horizontal samples and caches them.

82
00:05:52,169 --> 00:05:55,369
Those same samples can be reused for every row.

83
00:05:55,720 --> 00:05:59,859
For each of the eighteen plasma rows, it adds a vertical sample.

84
00:06:00,210 --> 00:06:04,690
Shifting the sum right by one gives an index from zero to thirty-one.

85
00:06:05,041 --> 00:06:09,867
That index selects two things: a colour from the palette table, and a dither

86
00:06:09,867 --> 00:06:11,591
character from the glyph table.

87
00:06:11,942 --> 00:06:14,289
Colour R A M receives the colour.

88
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:17,328
Screen memory receives the character number.

89
00:06:17,679 --> 00:06:22,117
The VIC-two combines them with the character set to draw the image.

90
00:06:22,468 --> 00:06:27,140
Notice the separate horizontal and vertical increments: five and nine.

91
00:06:27,491 --> 00:06:31,246
They control how tightly the waves repeat across the display.

92
00:06:31,597 --> 00:06:35,800
The phase changes over time, so those waves flow through each other.

93
00:06:36,151 --> 00:06:40,311
The store addresses advance by forty bytes at the end of each row.

94
00:06:40,663 --> 00:06:46,487
This kernel patches its own absolute store operands, so it belongs in writable memory.

95
00:06:46,838 --> 00:06:53,330
We disable interrupts in this small standalone demo; a larger program would need an

96
00:06:53,330 --> 00:06:55,649
explicit interrupt and memory-ownership design.

97
00:06:56,939 --> 00:06:58,731
Next, find scroll text.

98
00:06:59,669 --> 00:07:05,858
The message is an ordinary C string, written in uppercase because our supplied font

99
00:07:05,858 --> 00:07:07,627
defines those character positions.

100
00:07:08,565 --> 00:07:13,846
Screen codes and character shapes are separate: the screen holds an index, and the

101
00:07:13,846 --> 00:07:16,864
character set supplies the pixels at that index.

102
00:07:17,803 --> 00:07:22,880
Every third animation update, we move the bottom text row one character to the

103
00:07:22,880 --> 00:07:23,243
left.

104
00:07:24,181 --> 00:07:27,765
Then we put the next message character into the rightmost cell.

105
00:07:28,704 --> 00:07:34,059
When the string reaches its zero terminator, the message position returns to the beginning.

106
00:07:34,997 --> 00:07:38,752
This is character scrolling, so it moves eight pixels at a time.

107
00:07:39,691 --> 00:07:42,805
It is intentionally simple and easy to adapt.

108
00:07:43,744 --> 00:07:49,401
A pixel-smooth scroller would also coordinate fine scrolling and a raster split; that is

109
00:07:49,401 --> 00:07:50,613
a separate lesson.

110
00:07:51,552 --> 00:07:55,093
Change a few words in the message to make the demo yours.

111
00:07:56,032 --> 00:07:59,083
Keep the new text inside the quotation marks.

112
00:08:00,021 --> 00:08:04,629
The counter controls reading speed independently of the plasma phase.

113
00:08:05,568 --> 00:08:09,237
A larger threshold makes the text advance less frequently.

114
00:08:10,176 --> 00:08:15,061
After an edit, save and build before judging the new result in the emulator.

115
00:08:16,204 --> 00:08:18,444
The key asset handoff is in main.

116
00:08:18,647 --> 00:08:23,618
We include the Web sixty four assets header and the generated assets header.

117
00:08:23,822 --> 00:08:29,303
The generated header gives us Aurora asset, a descriptor for the character set stored

118
00:08:29,303 --> 00:08:30,478
in the project.

119
00:08:30,681 --> 00:08:36,736
Web sixty four asset copy charset takes two arguments: the destination character R A

120
00:08:36,736 --> 00:08:40,196
M address, and a pointer to that descriptor.

121
00:08:40,399 --> 00:08:44,581
It validates the character data description and copies the asset.

122
00:08:44,784 --> 00:08:47,686
We check its status before enabling the display.

123
00:08:47,889 --> 00:08:50,769
A red border is our simple failure signal.

124
00:08:50,973 --> 00:08:57,145
The runtime helper copies bytes; the program still chooses the VIC bank and memory

125
00:08:57,145 --> 00:08:57,586
layout.

126
00:08:57,790 --> 00:09:03,382
Here, the memory setup value selects screen memory at zero four hundred and characters

127
00:09:03,382 --> 00:09:04,979
at three eight hundred.

128
00:09:05,183 --> 00:09:09,831
We clear the screen and colour memory, draw the captions, and then turn the

129
00:09:09,831 --> 00:09:10,495
display on.

130
00:09:10,698 --> 00:09:16,906
The SDK also provides assembly include helpers for its runtime calling convention.

131
00:09:17,110 --> 00:09:20,353
C callers use the typed declaration directly.

132
00:09:20,556 --> 00:09:26,070
If you later move this setup into assembly, use the matching preparation or call

133
00:09:26,070 --> 00:09:30,796
macro from the assets include rather than guessing how arguments are passed.

134
00:09:31,482 --> 00:09:33,957
Before the final build, review Settings.

135
00:09:34,439 --> 00:09:39,038
This project uses the Web sixty four C compiler and its stack A B

136
00:09:39,038 --> 00:09:39,367
I.

137
00:09:39,849 --> 00:09:46,258
We select the speed profile, enable the relevant optimisation switches, and request runtime dependencies

138
00:09:46,258 --> 00:09:50,836
so the linker can keep the helpers the program needs.

139
00:09:51,318 --> 00:09:56,507
The demo draws directly to screen memory, so standard input and output are set

140
00:09:56,507 --> 00:09:57,249
to None.

141
00:09:57,731 --> 00:10:02,723
The emulator display is set to one times: one-to-one scaling, not Fit.

142
00:10:03,205 --> 00:10:07,023
That keeps the pixel grid consistent throughout this tutorial.

143
00:10:07,506 --> 00:10:10,492
Return to Build Targets and choose Build Target.

144
00:10:10,974 --> 00:10:15,966
Read the actual result: the output name, load address, and diagnostics.

145
00:10:16,449 --> 00:10:21,697
A successful build tells us that the compiler and linker accepted the project.

146
00:10:22,179 --> 00:10:26,509
It does not by itself prove that our graphics or animation are correct.

147
00:10:26,991 --> 00:10:29,423
Start the P R G to test that next.

148
00:10:29,906 --> 00:10:35,175
The IDE loads and starts the compiled program in its integrated emulator.

149
00:10:35,657 --> 00:10:41,085
Look for all three parts: the Aurora title in our custom font, the changing

150
00:10:41,085 --> 00:10:44,574
plasma field, and the message advancing along the bottom.

151
00:10:45,057 --> 00:10:49,835
If an edit appears to do nothing, confirm that you built and started the

152
00:10:49,835 --> 00:10:50,518
intended target.

153
00:10:51,173 --> 00:10:53,519
Let the demonstration run for a moment.

154
00:10:53,692 --> 00:10:58,919
The two waves keep changing their relationship, so the field moves through different bands

155
00:10:58,919 --> 00:10:59,666
and curves.

156
00:10:59,838 --> 00:11:05,797
The ordered-dither characters add a second layer of variation without changing video mode or

157
00:11:05,797 --> 00:11:08,350
uploading a new bitmap every frame.

158
00:11:08,523 --> 00:11:10,720
Watch the text as well as the colours.

159
00:11:10,893 --> 00:11:16,004
It should remain readable, advance at a regular pace, and eventually wrap to the

160
00:11:16,004 --> 00:11:17,464
beginning of the message.

161
00:11:17,637 --> 00:11:19,706
The fixed captions should stay still.

162
00:11:19,879 --> 00:11:25,582
Those are different responsibilities in the program, and checking each one is more useful

163
00:11:25,582 --> 00:11:28,433
than simply seeing something appear on screen.

164
00:11:28,606 --> 00:11:33,364
The frame loop waits for a raster position before starting its next update.

165
00:11:33,536 --> 00:11:37,483
That gives the program a repeatable reference to the video signal.

166
00:11:37,656 --> 00:11:43,271
It is not a promise that every possible modification will fit inside one video

167
00:11:43,271 --> 00:11:43,672
frame.

168
00:11:43,844 --> 00:11:48,586
If you add work, measure again and test on the video standard you intend

169
00:11:48,586 --> 00:11:49,263
to support.

170
00:11:49,436 --> 00:11:51,399
Now save the native project.

171
00:11:51,571 --> 00:11:56,381
A useful final check is to reopen that saved file in a fresh workspace

172
00:11:56,381 --> 00:11:58,099
and run the build again.

173
00:11:58,272 --> 00:12:03,648
This catches missing assets and settings that only existed in the previous session.

174
00:12:03,821 --> 00:12:10,314
The accompanying project has also been compiled and run in an independent VICE verification,

175
00:12:10,314 --> 00:12:16,344
including checks that the character bytes match and both animations change over time.

176
00:12:16,516 --> 00:12:20,100
Keep the project file when sharing an editable example.

177
00:12:20,273 --> 00:12:26,376
The source handout and raw character file are helpful references, but the native project

178
00:12:26,376 --> 00:12:30,300
contains the linked assets, source, and build settings together.

179
00:12:30,473 --> 00:12:35,470
That is the file another Web sixty four user should open to continue the

180
00:12:35,470 --> 00:12:35,827
work.

181
00:12:36,318 --> 00:12:42,684
We have taken a C starter through asset import, memory planning, source integration, build,

182
00:12:42,684 --> 00:12:43,593
and execution.

183
00:12:43,911 --> 00:12:50,060
The finished result combines a custom font, sixteen dither patterns, two animated waves, and

184
00:12:50,060 --> 00:12:51,378
a character scroller.

185
00:12:51,696 --> 00:12:55,173
For a first variation, change the text and the palette.

186
00:12:55,491 --> 00:12:59,651
For a second, adjust the horizontal and vertical wave increments.

187
00:12:59,970 --> 00:13:03,532
Change one thing at a time, build, and watch what it does.

188
00:13:03,850 --> 00:13:09,209
Keeping a working saved project makes it easy to compare versions and return to

189
00:13:09,209 --> 00:13:10,357
a known result.

190
00:13:10,675 --> 00:13:15,240
The most reusable idea is the separation of responsibilities.

191
00:13:15,559 --> 00:13:18,332
The native asset editor owns the graphics.

192
00:13:18,650 --> 00:13:20,997
Generated bindings describe them.

193
00:13:21,315 --> 00:13:25,091
Runtime helpers perform the documented asset transfer.

194
00:13:25,409 --> 00:13:28,801
Your program chooses the memory layout and the effect.

195
00:13:29,119 --> 00:13:35,332
Build Targets describes the executable output, and the emulator lets you test that output

196
00:13:35,332 --> 00:13:35,775
immediately.

197
00:13:36,094 --> 00:13:41,064
The supplied project, teaching files, and notes are included with this tutorial.

198
00:13:41,382 --> 00:13:46,639
Open the project, explore the code, and use it as a starting point for

199
00:13:46,639 --> 00:13:48,892
your own Commodore sixty four experiments.

200
00:13:49,210 --> 00:13:50,682
Thank you for watching.
